 ## Overview
-This method enables multi-view mini-batch training for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), addressing the limitation where standard 3DGS is restricted to single-view training due to rasterization overhead and incompatible density control mechanisms. Unlike single-view approaches that process one image per iteration, this method renders multiple images simultaneously to reduce the variance of mini-batch gradients, leading to more stable optimization comparable to Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S1, unofficial]. It achieves this through a thread-efficient partial rendering scheme that mitigates the idle thread overhead inherent in tile-based rasterizers and introduces a 3D-space compatible D-SSIM loss [S1, unofficial].
+This method enables stable optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by processing multiple camera views per training iteration, addressing the high variance of gradients inherent in standard single-view training. It distinguishes itself from standard 3DGS by replacing the single-image mini-batch with a multi-image batch, while overcoming the rasterization overhead and adaptive density control (ADC) incompatibilities that typically prevent such an approach.
 
 ## How it works
+### Inputs and Outputs
+**Inputs:**
+- A set of $N$ training images with corresponding camera poses.
+- The current 3D Gaussian scene representation (means $\mu$, covariances $\Sigma$, colors $c$, opacities $o$).
+- Hyperparameters: learning rate $\eta_t$, number of sampled mini-batches $K$ for variance estimation.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
